      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Interesting approach - the 5-activation LUT is a clever way to amortize the table lookup overhead.\n\nOne alternative that's worked well in our implementation (Project Zero, https://github.com/shifulegend/project-zero) is combining VBMI + VNNI rather than a pure LUT path:\n\n1. `vpermb` replicates each packed byte 4x across a 512-bit register (one instruction to expand 16 packed bytes to 64 weight-encoding bytes)\n2. `vpmultishiftqb` extracts the individual 2-bit codes per-slot\n3. The resulting int8 weight encodings feed directly into VNNI dot products\n\nThe VBMI 3-instruction unpack vs SSE fallback is about 2.7x in our microbenchmarks. Combined with VNNI and PGO/LTO we get 36.25 tok/s on BitNet b1.58-2B-4T (4-core Xeon Emerald Rapids): https://openbenchmarking.org/result/2606207-SHIF-PROJECT42\n\nThe main trade-off with VBMI+VNNI vs a LUT path: VBMI requires Ice Lake or newer (no Cascade Lake VNNI support), while a LUT can work on any AVX-512F machine. The 5-activation LUT you're describing sidesteps the VNNI requirement entirely, which is a meaningful portability win for older AVX-512 hardware.\n\nHave you benchmarked the LUT path on hardware without VNNI? Curious whether the 5-activation grouping gives better throughput than VBMI+VNNI on Cascade Lake specifically.",
