      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "The Intel vs AMD VNNI throughput gap is probably what's driving this.\n\nIntel Sapphire/Emerald Rapids runs VPDPBUSD 512-bit at roughly twice the throughput of AMD Zen 4 - two execution ports vs one per cycle (uops.info has the breakdown). Zen 5 (your 9950X) I haven't confirmed, but if VNNI-heavy code is hitting a compute ceiling on AMD, the same code on our Xeon would have a higher ceiling.\n\nAt 1 thread the LUT wins by 1.25x on your hardware. If AMD's lower VNNI throughput is artificially capping our VNNI path, the comparison on Emerald Rapids might look different - that's the hypothesis anyway, worth testing rather than assuming.\n\nYour 8 to 16 thread numbers are interesting: 4.5 tok/s gain (52.13 to 56.66). That's memory bandwidth pressure, not compute - our 4-core Xeon shows the same step pattern.\n\nI'll pull ternary_mm_avx512.cpp and run the 5-activation LUT against our VNNI kernel on the Xeon. Is that HEAD or is there a branch? I want bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T for the model so numbers line up.",
