      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "Yes, LUT wins on Intel too.\n\nRan lut_mm's bench.exe on a Xeon @ 2.80 GHz (avx512f/bw/vnni/dq/vl, 4 cores, GCC 13.3.0, Linux) before doing the full integration into PZ. Default shape M=256, K=2080, N=2048:\n\n| threads | impl | Gop/s | vs VNNI |\n|---------|------|-------|---------|\n| 1 | dense_mm_vnni | 132.6 | 1.00x |\n| 1 | lut_mm_avx512 | 153.7 | 1.16x |\n| 2 | lut_mm_avx512_mt | 281.7 | 2.12x |\n| 4 | lut_mm_avx512_mt | 573.9 | 4.33x |\n\n1.16x at T=1, vs your 1.25x on Zen 5. The margin being slightly lower on Intel is kind of interesting actually - it doesn't line up with my earlier hypothesis about dual-ported VNNI being a bigger ceiling. Might be that this Xeon's vpermt2w characteristics put it closer to AMD than I expected, or just VM/frequency effects.\n\nThese are kernel Gop/s, not tok/s, so not directly comparable to your inference numbers. Multi-thread scaling is basically linear (3.73x on 4 cores at T=4), consistent with the compute-bound claim in the README.\n\nSide note: README flags 'this GCC/Clang branch has never been built' - compiled fine on first try, might want to update that.",
